## Prerequisites

### Package Installation

The following packages are installed:

```bash
npm
nodejs version 22+
```

To install these packages, run:

```bash
# Install npm
sudo apt install npm

# Install nodejs
curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_22.x | sudo -E bash -
sudo apt install -y nodejs
```

### Create a VS Code Publisher

To create a .vsix file, you must specify a publisher for your extension. The following link provides instructions on how to set up a publisher.

[Create a Publisher](https://code.visualstudio.com/api/working-with-extensions/publishing-extension#create-a-publisher){class="flux-button _3"}


## 1. Install the VS Code Extension Packaging Tool


Command:

```bash
sudo npm install -g @vscode/vsce
```

Example Successful Output:

```bash
added 314 packages in 11s
```

### Installation Warnings

If your system `Node.js` version is older than what vsce requires, npm will emit engine warnings during installation. These warnings indicate that the current `Node.js` version does not meet the minimum version expected by the package.



### Permission Error

When installing `vsce` globally without elevated permissions, npm may throw a permission-related error. The example below shows the type of message you might see:

Example Input:

```bash
npm install -g @vscode/vsce
```

Example Error Output:

```
npm ERR! code EACCES
npm ERR! syscall mkdir
npm ERR! path /usr/local/lib/node_modules
...
npm ERR! The operation was rejected by your operating system.
npm ERR! It is likely you do not have the permissions to access this file as the current user
...
```

---

## 3. Package the Extension

In the root of your theme directory (where `package.json` is located), run:

```bash
vsce package
```

### File is Not Defined Error

When running `vsce` package, you may encounter a runtime error originating from the `undici` dependency. This typically occurs when the installed version of `vsce` depends on newer Web API implementations that are not available in the system’s `Node.js` version. In these cases, the process fails before completing the `.vsix` packaging step and prints a stack trace similar to the one below.

```
/usr/local/lib/node_modules/@vscode/vsce/node_modules/undici/lib/web/webidl/index.js:533
webidl.is.File = webidl.util.MakeTypeAssertion(File)
                                               ^

ReferenceError: File is not defined
    at Object.<anonymous> (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/@vscode/vsce/node_modules/undici/lib/web/webidl/index.js:533:48)
    at Module._compile (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1356:14)
    at Module._extensions..js (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1414:10)
    at Module.load (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1197:32)
    at Module._load (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1013:12)
    at Module.require (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1225:19)
    at require (node:internal/modules/helpers:177:18)
    at Object.<anonymous> (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/@vscode/vsce/node_modules/undici/lib/web/fetch/util.js:12:20)
    at Module._compile (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1356:14)
    at Module._extensions..js (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1414:10)

Node.js v18.19.1
```






This will generate a `.vsix` file named like:

```
lagomorphic-constructs-theme-pack-0.0.1.vsix
```

The name and version come from your `package.json`.

---

### 3. Install the `.vsix` in VS Code

1. Open the Command Palette:
   `Ctrl+Shift+P` (Windows/Linux) or `Cmd+Shift+P` (macOS)
2. Run: **Extensions: Install from VSIX…**
3. Select the generated `.vsix` file
4. Apply the theme via:
   **Preferences → Color Theme**

---

### Optional: Generate via VS Code UI (No Terminal Needed)

VS Code also supports packaging through the Command Palette:

1. `Ctrl+Shift+P`
2. Run: **Developer: Generate VSIX from Extension…**
3. Select your extension folder
4. The `.vsix` file will be generated automatically

---

### Notes

- Your `package.json` must include a valid `publisher` field, e.g.:

```json
"publisher": "Rebecca Jennifer"
```

- A personal access token is **not required** unless you plan to publish to the Marketplace.